{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789399402,"nonce":"A/+sIjBy9roD2sEZLLwgMkxQ18w=","title":"Object, string and sequence transfer: what a crossing costs and leaves behind","message":"benches/transfer.rs times five crossings each way, Haxe→Wren and Wren→Haxe, over the interop fixture (Bench.hx, tally.wren): the same object crossing again, a fresh one each call, one the callee makes and the caller drops, a string in and out, and a hundred-element sequence walked on the other side. Beside the time per iteration it prints the bytes the core heap handed out per iteration and the collections and Wren cycles the run took, so garbage per crossing is visible.\n\nFirst run (2026-09-14, 100k iterations, load ~6), ns / bytes per iteration:\n\n| crossing | Haxe→Wren | Wren→Haxe |\n|---|---|---|\n| same object | 171 / 16 | 39 / 0 |\n| fresh object | 463 / 128 | 257 / 160 |\n| returned object | 261 / 160 | 454 / 128 |\n| string | 314 / 128 | 338 / 128 |\n| sequence, per element | 264 / 16 | 480 / 16 |\n\nWhat the profile showed and what was done the same afternoon:\n\n- Every Haxe object crossing decoded its class name from UTF-16 into a String, twice: is_string (obj_name == \"String\") and is_face_type walking the superclass chain by name. Both compare in place now, and String is known by its type pointer once the program attaches. Same object 171 → 86.\n- Every Haxe object entering Wren paid a heap lookup under the GC lock (owns_start, to tell a ref coming home from a wrapper) before the stand-in map was consulted. The map is consulted first now; only a native seen for the first time costs the lookup. Same object 86 → 63.\n- Every new object's class was found through the protocol's type_name, a fresh core Str per call, converted to a Rust String and SipHashed against a (lang, String) map. type_name answers an interned Symbol now (Ash keeps one per hl_type, Wren interns the export's name), the map is keyed by (lang, Symbol), and bridge::type_name is the string form over it. Fresh object 384 → 225, returned 381 → 250.\n\nLeft, by cost: the string cells (~280 ns, 128 B: a core Str, a Haxe String and a Wren string per crossing), and the sequences (~260–330 ns per element: Wren's iterate and iteratorValue are two host calls per element, each a protocol send with no site; Haxe's Sequence does length and [] per element the same way; index on a Haxe ArrayBytes goes through getDyn, which boxes the element). The 16 B a same-object Haxe→Wren crossing leaves is the HaxeRef wrapper made at the boundary and dropped after the stand-in is found.","files":null}]}